Use ASP.NET Core's **`WebApplicationFactory<T>`** — it hosts your app in-process with a test `HttpClient`, no real port needed.

### Setup

```csharp
// Add these NuGet packages to your test project:
// Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing
// ModelContextProtocol  (official C# MCP SDK)
```

### Example: Full MCP protocol test

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing;
using ModelContextProtocol.Client;
using ModelContextProtocol.Transport;

public class McpIntegrationTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
{
    private readonly WebApplicationFactory<Program> _factory;

    public McpIntegrationTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
    {
        _factory = factory;
    }

    [Fact]
    public async Task CanInitializeAndCallTool()
    {
        // WebApplicationFactory gives you an in-memory HttpClient
        var httpClient = _factory.CreateClient();

        // Use the MCP SDK's SseClientTransport with your test client
        var transport = new SseClientTransport(
            new SseClientTransportOptions
            {
                Endpoint = new Uri(httpClient.BaseAddress!, "/mcp"),
                // or "/sse" depending on your server's endpoint
            },
            // Pass the factory's HttpClient so no real network call is made
            httpClient
        );

        await using var mcpClient = await McpClientFactory.CreateAsync(
            transport,
            cancellationToken: CancellationToken.None
        );

        // Now test real MCP operations
        var tools = await mcpClient.ListToolsAsync();
        Assert.NotEmpty(tools);

        var result = await mcpClient.CallToolAsync(
            "your-tool-name",
            new Dictionary<string, object?> { ["param1"] = "value1" }
        );

        Assert.False(result.IsError);
        Assert.Contains("expected output", result.Content[0].Text);
    }
}
```

### Key points

- **`WebApplicationFactory<Program>`** boots your entire middleware pipeline (auth, routing, MCP endpoints) in-memory.
- **`SseClientTransport`** accepts an existing `HttpClient`, so it uses the test server's handler — no TCP socket needed.
- If your server uses **Streamable HTTP** instead of SSE, use `StreamableHttpClientTransport` the same way.
- You get full protocol coverage: initialize handshake, `tools/list`, `tools/call`, etc.

### If you need to override services

```csharp
var factory = _factory.WithWebHostBuilder(builder =>
{
    builder.ConfigureServices(services =>
    {
        // Swap in test doubles
        services.AddSingleton<IMyDependency, FakeMyDependency>();
    });
});
var httpClient = factory.CreateClient();
```

This gives you true end-to-end MCP protocol testing (JSON-RPC framing, SSE streaming, content negotiation) with zero network overhead.
